# Target C-II — projected diameter-stress dimension is exactly the row-nullity on the contact tangent

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only exact structural identity; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** canonical full-core Type-II positive-contact core with actual loaded diameter graph `H`  
**Depends on:** canonical mixed rigidity rank; exact pure-contact stress/closure-corank identity; projected diameter-stress dimension theorem; relative-interior KKT stress  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current defect partition uses

\[
k=d_H+p_D+\sigma_C,
\]

where `d_H=q-h` is the Woodall diameter-edge deficit, `sigma_C` is pure-contact
self-stress dimension, and `p_D` is the affine dimension of the normalized projected
diameter-stress polytope.  The quantity `p_D` has so far been treated mainly as a stress
parameter.  It has an exact primal interpretation.

Let

\[
V_C:=\ker R_C/\mathcal E
\]

be the contact infinitesimal-flex space modulo the three Euclidean motions, and let

\[
G:V_C\longrightarrow\mathbb R^h
\]

be the derivative map of the `h` actual diameter bars, obtained by restricting the
diameter rigidity rows to contact infinitesimal flexes.  Then

\[
\boxed{G\text{ is injective}}
\tag{TD.1}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\ker G^T=\pi_H\bigl(\ker R_{C\cup H}^T\bigr).
}
\tag{TD.2}
\]

Consequently, if

\[
d_C:=\dim V_C,
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
h-d_C=p_D+1,}
\tag{TD.3}
\]

or equivalently

\[
\boxed{d_C=h-p_D-1.}
\tag{TD.4}
\]

Since `d_H=q-h`, this gives the second exact identity

\[
\boxed{
d_H+p_D=q-d_C-1.}
\tag{TD.5}
\]

Thus the entire currently unconverted defect `d_H+p_D` is exactly the codimension of
the full contact tangent inside the `q-1` diameter-row budget.  It is not merely a sum
of two bookkeeping quantities.

Combining (TD.5) with the current correlated weighted-seed frontier rewrites the live
remaining obstruction directly in tangent-space language.  Since `q/D->pi`, every live
terminal survivor satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\pi-{d_C\over D}
>
\max\left\{
5.9765850-4{m_D\over D},
\ 1.5158692-{8\over15}{m_D\over D},
\ 0.6733591
\right\}-o(1).
}
\tag{TD.6}
\]

Here `m_D` is the dimension of the explicit exact finite contact-preserving manifold,
whereas `d_C` is the complete infinitesimal contact-flex dimension.  Equation (TD.6)
therefore measures the remaining nonlinear integration gap without introducing a new
parameter.

A useful endpoint occurs when `p_D=0`.  Then

\[
\dim\ker G^T=1.
\]

The relative-interior KKT diameter vector has every coordinate positive and spans this
one-dimensional kernel.  Therefore the `h` diameter derivative rows on `V_C` form a
positive linear circuit:

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}G=h-1,
\quad
\ker G^T=\mathbb R\beta,
\quad
\beta_e>0\ (e\in H),
}
\tag{TD.7}
\]

and every set of `h-1` diameter derivative rows is linearly independent.  More
generally, `p_D+1` is exactly the linear row-nullity of the actual-diameter derivative
system on the full contact tangent.

This does not close C-II.  It identifies the exact linear object that a closing theorem
has to constrain: the straight-line diameter thrackle gives `h` covectors on the
contact tangent, injectively locking that tangent, with row-nullity precisely `p_D+1`.
The unresolved `d_H+p_D` is exactly `q-d_C-1`.

---

## 1. The diameter derivative map on contact flexes

Let the nonisolated positive-contact core have `v` vertices, contact rigidity matrix
`R_C`, and actual-diameter rigidity matrix `R_H`.  Euclidean infinitesimal motions lie
in `ker R_C` and are also killed by every distance derivative, so `R_H` descends to a
well-defined map

\[
G:V_C=\ker R_C/\mathcal E\to\mathbb R^h.
\]

Canonical mixed rigidity gives

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}=2v-3.
\]

If a class `[u] in V_C` lies in `ker G`, then

\[
R_Cu=0,
\qquad
R_Hu=0.
\]

Thus `u` is an infinitesimal flex of the full mixed active framework.  Full mixed rank
forces `u` to be Euclidean, so `[u]=0` in `V_C`.  Therefore `G` is injective, proving
(TD.1), and

\[
\operatorname{rank}G=d_C.
\tag{1.1}
\]

---

## 2. The projected mixed-stress space is exactly `ker G^T`

Let

\[
\mathcal L:=\ker R_{C\cup H}^T
\]

be the signed mixed equilibrium-stress space, and write a mixed stress as
`(alpha,beta)` with contact coordinates `alpha` and diameter coordinates `beta`.

If `(alpha,beta) in mathcal L`, then

\[
R_C^T\alpha+R_H^T\beta=0.
\]

For every contact infinitesimal flex `u in ker R_C`,

\[
\beta^TR_Hu=-\alpha^TR_Cu=0.
\]

Hence the projected diameter vector `beta` annihilates the image of `G`, so

\[
\pi_H(\mathcal L)\subseteq\ker G^T.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Conversely, suppose `beta in ker G^T`.  Then

\[
\beta^TR_Hu=0
\qquad(u\in\ker R_C).
\]

Therefore `R_H^T beta` lies in the orthogonal complement of `ker R_C`.  By finite
dimensional linear algebra,

\[
(\ker R_C)^\perp=\operatorname{im}R_C^T.
\]

So there is an `alpha` such that

\[
R_C^T\alpha=-R_H^T\beta.
\]

Thus `(alpha,beta) in mathcal L`, and

\[
\ker G^T\subseteq\pi_H(\mathcal L).
\tag{2.2}
\]

Equations (2.1)--(2.2) prove (TD.2).

No sign condition is used here.  This is an identity of signed linear stress spaces.

---

## 3. Dimension identity

The hostile-audited projected-stress theorem gives

\[
\dim\pi_H(\mathcal L)=s_Z+1-\sigma_C=p_D+1,
\tag{3.1}
\]

where the final equality is exactly the definition of the normalized projected
polytope's affine dimension, justified by the full-support relative-interior KKT stress.

By (TD.2) and (1.1),

\[
\dim\pi_H(\mathcal L)
=\dim\ker G^T
=h-\operatorname{rank}G
=h-d_C.
\]

Comparing with (3.1) gives

\[
h-d_C=p_D+1,
\]

which proves (TD.3)--(TD.4).

Now use `d_H=q-h`:

\[
\begin{aligned}
d_H+p_D
&=q-h+p_D\\
&=q-(d_C+p_D+1)+p_D\\
&=q-d_C-1.
\end{aligned}
\]

This proves (TD.5).

For consistency, the exact contact-flex formula gives the same result:

\[
d_C=T-2f-1+\sigma_C=(q-k)-1+\sigma_C,
\]

while `k=d_H+p_D+sigma_C`.

---

## 4. Positive-circuit endpoint when `p_D=0`

If `p_D=0`, equation (TD.3) gives

\[
h=d_C+1,
\qquad
\dim\ker G^T=1.
\]

The canonical relative-interior KKT stress projects to a vector

\[
\beta\in\ker G^T
\]

with every actual-diameter coefficient strictly positive.  Because the kernel is
one-dimensional, every linear dependence among the `h` diameter derivative rows is a
multiple of this full-support positive dependence.

If some `h-1` rows were dependent, extending that dependence by zero on the omitted row
would produce a nonzero vector in `ker G^T` with one zero coordinate.  It could not be a
multiple of the full-support `beta`.  Contradiction.  Therefore every `h-1` rows are
independent and the complete row family is a positive circuit, proving (TD.7).

This statement concerns derivative rows on the full contact tangent.  It does not claim
that all `d_C` infinitesimal contact flexes integrate to exact finite motions.

---

## 5. Live frontier in tangent language

The current correlated weighted-seed note gives

\[
{d_H+p_D\over D}
>
\max\left\{
5.9765850-4{m_D\over D},
\ 1.5158692-{8\over15}{m_D\over D},
\ 0.6733591
\right\}-o(1).
\tag{5.1}
\]

From (TD.5) and `q/D->pi`,

\[
{d_H+p_D\over D}
=\pi-{d_C\over D}+o(1).
\tag{5.2}
\]

Substitution gives (TD.6).  No new numerical approximation is needed.

Two interpretations are exact enough to be useful for the next step.

1. `p_D` is the row-nullity minus one of the diameter derivative system on the full
   contact tangent.  Bounding projected-stress dimension is therefore the same linear
   problem as bounding excess diameter-row dependence after restriction to contact
   flexes.
2. `d_H+p_D` is the loss from the maximal possible `q-1` contact-tangent dimension.
   The current scalar/topological frontier is already a statement about the complete
   contact tangent, not merely about an auxiliary stress polytope.

The remaining gap is nonlinear: only an explicit `m_D`-dimensional submanifold of this
`d_C`-dimensional contact tangent is presently integrated exactly in the singular
closure-corank cases.  Either a stronger integration theorem or a direct obstruction
to positive diameter locking on the full tangent would attack the remaining C-II
branch without introducing another scalar defect.

---

## 6. Scope audit

- `R_C`, `R_H`, and `G` use only the nonisolated positive-contact core.  Contact-isolated
  source points remain in the source and are not inserted into the rigidity matrices.
- The equality `pi_H(mathcal L)=ker G^T` is signed linear algebra.  Nonnegativity enters
  only in the `p_D=0` positive-circuit corollary through the known full-support KKT
  stress.
- No finite nonlinear integration is inferred from infinitesimal contact-flex
  dimension.
- No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** proved the exact contact-tangent/projected-stress duality (TD.1)--(TD.5)
and isolated `p_D=0` as a full-support positive circuit of diameter derivatives.  C-II
remains open.